.\" Man page for dsa-quest(7) \(em concepts behind DSA Quest.
.\"
.\" Hyphenation and justification are off on purpose so that every statement
.\" survives `man 7 dsa-quest | grep <keyword>` on one unbroken line.
.TH DSA-QUEST 7 "2026-08-13" "dsa-quest 0.1.0" "Miscellaneous Information Manual"
.nh
.ad l
.
.SH NAME
dsa-quest \- the learning model, scheduler and content format behind dsa(1)
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
This page explains why
.BR dsa (1)
behaves as it does.
For how to run it, see
.BR dsa (1).
.PP
The application exists to answer one question repeatedly until the answer is
automatic:
.RS 4
Can I recognise this pattern when the problem does not tell me which to use?
.RE
.PP
Everything below follows from that. A tool optimised for solving problems you
already know the category of trains the wrong half of the skill.
.
.SH THE THREE DIMENSIONS
Each pattern owns three independently scheduled memory states.
.TP 18
.B recognition
Trained by Pattern Hunter and Pattern Duel.
.TP
.B recall
Trained by Pattern Recall.
.TP
.B implementation
Trained by Code Completion and Boss Fight.
.PP
They are separate because they decay at genuinely different rates. Recognition
survives longest; implementation goes first. Averaging them into one number
produces a figure that describes none of your three abilities and schedules
you
on it.
.PP
Three further numbers are
.I derived
from the event log and never scheduled.
.TP 18
.B speed
Median solve time against par, capped at 1.0.
.TP
.B accuracy
First\-attempt success rate over a rolling window.
.TP
.B retention
The
.I minimum
retrievability across the three dimensions, not the mean. A pattern you can
name
but not write is not partly retained; it is one you cannot use.
.
.SH MASTERY
Overall mastery blends the three scheduled dimensions only.
.PP
.nf
recognition     0.30
recall          0.25
implementation  0.45
.fi
.PP
Speed and accuracy are displayed but excluded from the blend. They already fed
the rating rubric that produced those memory states, and folding them in again
would count the same evidence twice.
.PP
Within a dimension, consolidation outweighs current recall probability:
.PP
.nf
score = 0.65 * maturity + 0.35 * retrievability
maturity = min(1, stability_days / 21)
.fi
.PP
Retrievability swings between roughly 1.0 just after a review and the target
retention just before the next one. Weighting it heavily would make mastery
oscillate visibly from day to day and make unlocks flicker.
.
.SH THE IMPLEMENTATION GATE
Two rules guard one claim: mastery means you can
.I use
the pattern, and you cannot use a pattern you cannot write.
.TP 4
1.
Overall is capped at 0.60 until implementation has at least 3 reviews.
Recognising a pattern in a four\-way multiple choice is not knowing it.
.TP
2.
Overall may never exceed implementation's own score by more than 0.15.
.PP
Rule 1 alone counts
.I attempts,
not successes. A learner who failed six implementation reviews still clears
it,
and would unlock dependent patterns while unable to write a line. Rule 2
closes
that.
.PP
Observed behaviour:
.PP
.nf
implementation always fails       overall 0.50   unlocks no
implementation effortful but right overall 0.54  unlocks no
implementation solid              overall 0.96   unlocks yes
.fi
.
.SH SCHEDULING
The scheduler is FSRS\-6 (Free Spaced Repetition Scheduler), one card per
pattern per dimension.
.SS Why not SM-2
SM\-2 compresses a card's whole memory into a single ease factor. Every lapse
ratchets that factor down and nothing lifts it back up, so difficult material
spirals into ever\-shorter intervals \(em the documented
.I ease hell.
It also cannot say how likely you are to recall something right now, because
it
models no forgetting curve, only a multiplier.
.SS Why FSRS
FSRS models three quantities explicitly \(em difficulty, stability and
retrievability \(em under a power\-law forgetting curve fitted to very large
review
corpora. Two consequences matter here.
.TP 4
\(bu
You state a target retention and it schedules to hit it, instead of you
guessing interval multipliers.
.TP
\(bu
Retrievability is a real probability of recall right now, which is exactly the
retention figure the mastery screen needs. It falls out of the model rather
than
being invented.
.PP
Default target retention is 0.90. Lower means longer intervals and more
forgetting; higher means more reviews for the same material.
.
.SH RATINGS
FSRS requires a grade after every review: again, hard, good or easy. In
flashcard
software the user supplies it. Here it is derived, because a learner grading
their own code grades it generously.
.TP
.B recognition
Being right on a four\-way choice is cheap, so speed is the evidence. Under
0.5x
par is easy; over 1.5x par is hard. A second guess is elimination, not recall,
and caps at hard.
.TP
.B recall
Self\-graded, with an objective floor. Missing an essential rubric point
forces
again regardless of what you clicked. Be generous with yourself about details,
not about the invariant.
.TP
.B implementation
A compiler judges it, so honesty is free. Not accepted is again. Hints or more
than two tries cap at hard. Under 0.6x par with no hints is easy.
.PP
A judge failure produces
.I no rating at all.
The card is untouched and nothing is logged. Our bug must never become your
lapse.
.PP
A lapse means forgetting something you had learned: an
.I again
on a card that had reached the review state. Failing during initial learning
is
not a lapse, or every new pattern would look fragile.
.
.SH XP AND INCENTIVES
The design risk in gamifying a study tool is that the fun thing and the useful
thing diverge. Grinding your favourite pattern feels good and teaches nothing.
Two multipliers close that gap.
.TP
.B Diminishing returns
The k\-th exercise on one pattern in a day is worth 0.5 to the power (k\-1),
floored at 0.125. Breadth becomes the profitable strategy within three
repetitions.
.TP
.B Due-review bonus
Anything the scheduler marks due is worth 1.5x. Whatever FSRS most wants you
to
see is also the highest\-scoring thing available.
.PP
Measured effect on a medium solve:
.PP
.nf
already done 3x today          6 XP
fresh pattern                 50 XP
fresh pattern, due for review 75 XP
due plus a 30-day streak      94 XP
.fi
.PP
The greedy play is therefore the correct study plan, and the application never
has to nag.
.PP
XP is awarded from the
.I outcome,
never from the rating. If an honest
.I again
cost you XP, you would have a direct incentive to lie to the scheduler.
.PP
Nothing subtracts XP. A failed attempt on solve, boss or code completion pays
10 percent of base, because half an hour on a Boss Fight for literally nothing
teaches you to stop opening the application.
.
.SH UNLOCKS
Worlds are presentation. The prerequisite DAG is what actually gates content,
so
a pattern opens the moment its own prerequisites reach 0.65 mastery \(em you
are
never made to grind an unrelated corner of a world.
.PP
Unlocks are recorded, not recomputed. A prerequisite that later decays does
not
re\-lock what it opened. Withdrawing access because you forgot something would
punish honest reporting of a lapse.
.PP
Every locked pattern can state exactly what it is waiting for and how close
that
is, for example:
.RS 4
needs two-pointers at 48% of 65%, hashing-frequency at 12% of 65%
.RE
.
.SH ANTI-MEMORISATION
Static problems are memorised in about six weeks, after which the tool
measures
recall of answers rather than recognition of patterns.
.TP 4
\(bu
Distractors are drawn from a per\-pattern confusion graph, not at random.
Random
distractors make the question trivial: graph BFS beside sliding window on an
array problem answers itself.
.TP
\(bu
Distractors are ordered by
.I your
history. A pattern you have personally mistaken for this one is offered first,
so your wrong answers make later rounds harder.
.TP
\(bu
A wrong answer returns the discriminator for that specific ordered pair \(em
the
one question that separates them \(em rather than merely naming the right
answer.
.TP
\(bu
The classic problem each statement drills is revealed only
.I after
you answer. Seeing a problem number above a statement gives the pattern away.
.
.SH DIFFERENTIAL JUDGING
Code Completion has no authored answer key.
.PP
Holes are marked inline in the canonical template using comments, so the
template remains a single source of truth: it still compiles, still runs, and
still appears in the pattern reference, with no parallel exercise copy to
drift
out of sync.
.PP
.nf
// >>> HOLE id=shrink prompt=Restore the invariant after admitting a[r]
        while ((int)freq.size() > k) {
            if (--freq[a[l]] == 0) freq.erase(a[l]);
            ++l;
        }
// <<< HOLE
.fi
.PP
The intact template is the oracle. It is compiled, run on the sample inputs,
and
its output becomes the expected output for your version. A hole can be added,
moved or resized and no expected outputs need authoring or maintaining \(em
and the
answer key cannot disagree with the template, because it is derived from it.
.PP
Two properties are enforced for every hole in every shipped template: the
template's own code must be accepted, and an empty answer must be rejected.
The
second catches a hole placed around decorative code, where the exercise would
teach nothing.
.
.SH CONTENT FORMAT
Content is versioned YAML on disk. The database holds user state only, so
editing a problem never requires a migration and your progress survives
content
updates.
.PP
Adding a pattern file is sufficient to make it schedulable; cards are created
idempotently at startup.
.SS Pattern fields
.TP 18
.B signals
Cues in a statement that point here. Strength 3 is near\-decisive.
.TP
.B anti_signals
Cues that rule this out, and what they point to instead. Recognition is half
elimination, and that half is what learners are never taught.
.TP
.B confusable_with
Patterns this is mistaken for, each with a
.I tell:
the single question that discriminates them. Must be mutual \(em validation
fails
on a one\-way confusion.
.TP
.B invariant
One sentence: the thing that must stay true.
.TP
.B traps
Ways to get the pattern right and the code wrong, each with a symptom naming
which test fails and how.
.TP
.B recall_rubric
Concretely checkable points for self\-grading. Essential points cannot be
waved
through.
.PP
Validate a content tree with:
.PP
.nf
python -m dsaquest.content.validate -v
.fi
.PP
Cross\-file checks run at load time: unknown pattern references, one\-way
confusions, prerequisite cycles, missing templates, duplicate world slots, and
statements that name their own pattern.
.
.SH THREAT MODEL
Submitted code is assumed hostile. It must not read the home directory, reach
the network, exhaust the machine, or survive the judge.
.PP
Layer by layer, and what each catches that the others cannot:
.TP
.B TasksMax
The only real fork\-bomb defence.
.B RLIMIT_NPROC
is deliberately not used: it is enforced per\-UID, so a fork bomb in the
sandbox
would exhaust the limit for your entire login session and take down the
desktop
running the judge.
.TP
.B MemoryMax
A backstop preventing host OOM.
.B RLIMIT_AS
at the same value is what produces a clean attributable failure instead of an
opaque kill.
.TP
.B wall clock > CPU limit
A process blocked on a read burns wall time without burning CPU.
.B RLIMIT_CPU
alone would never fire on a program waiting for input that never arrives.
.TP
.B --new-session
Not optional. Without a fresh session the payload can inject keystrokes into
the
terminal that launched the judge.
.PP
Resource usage is read from the cgroup rather than from
.BR wait4 (2),
because bwrap does not propagate its child's rusage. Measured on the
development
machine, a process that used 1.54 s and 67.7 MB reported as 0.00 s and 7.0 MB
through wait4. The cgroup also accounts for the whole process tree, and its
OOM
counter turns memory\-kill detection from a heuristic into a fact.
.
